A||N||1||DaveL||Throttle Valve Cable||||5||
Z||000000||DaveL||02-28-2000||11:10 AM||||I've been having occasional trouble with my THM 125 in an 87 SE 2.5 DIS. It often drives and shifts perfectly. Occasionally it won't shift into 2nd or 3rd until it hits 3500 rpms. Fluid levels are normal, and I had the local Grease Monkey do a flush of the tranny (Wish I would've had the filter changed then too). They thought I was crazy since the fluid appeared normal. I suspect the throttle valve cable may be bad, but my Hayne's book doesn't have any illustrations of my setup, the 700 TBI and THM 125. There doesn't seem to be any provision for adjustment at all. The end at the TBI is simple to connect by hand. The other end on the trans has an assembly attached by two bolts. Is this a difficult thing to replace? Can the cable be adjusted? And could it cause this sort of prob?||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||02-28-2000||07:59 PM||||It can cause wierd problems yes. especially if it's sticking. you can try cleaning it up a bit.<P>take it off.... no it's not very hard as I recall. Then put the manifold end into a cup full of WD-40, you can get this in liquid as well as spray, and work the cable from the other end for awhile. that should help clear oil or dirt from it. The closer to flat you can keep the cable the easier it will work in the WD-40. Afterwards try to shake out the excess.||||reg||1
